/-!
# Ω_{F/K} is spanned by dx for separating x

Theorem statement: `KaehlerDifferential.span_D_eq_top_of_transcendental`
(`cff114e0-913d-5ab8-a133-347de5198cee`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `76fe99e6-1761-5fa8-80c6-b680ac94900b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field, $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and $x \in F$ an element
transcendental over $K$, i.e. the evaluation map $K[X] \to F$ at $x$ is injective. Assume further
that $F$ is separable as an algebra over the intermediate field $K(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin K
{x}` generated by $x$. Then the $F$-submodule of the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$
spanned by the single element $D_{K,F}(x) = dx$ is all of $\Omega_{F/K}$; equivalently, every
differential of $F$ over $K$ is of the form $g\,dx$ with $g \in F$. The assertion is only the
spanning statement: nothing is claimed here about $dx$ being nonzero, so linear independence, and
hence one-dimensionality of $\Omega_{F/K}$, is not part of the conclusion.

This is the classical description of the differentials of a separably generated function field of
one variable: $\Omega_{F/K}$ is generated by the differential of a separating element. Together with
the companion statement that $dx \neq 0$ for such an $x$, it underlies the treatment of
differentials at a place of an algebraic curve, where it is used to define and compute differential
coefficients.

